    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, \"human-induced friction\" in this context is heavily tied to the cognitive load—the mental effort required for drivers to make split-second decisions, especially when merging or yielding at the entry points. When a driver hesitates, it's not just a slow reaction; it creates a ripple effect because the *following* drivers must immediately recalibrate their speed and gap acceptance based on that unexpected pause. This creates a localized breakdown in the smooth flow that feels disproportionate to the initial hesitation.\n\nIn modeling, this chaotic element is often classified under **stochastic variability** or **driver aggressiveness/caution parameters**. Instead of a single, fixed gap acceptance time, you model a *distribution* of gap acceptance times, where the variance of that distribution represents the unpredictability. A high variance means some drivers are overly cautious (creating delays) while others are aggressive (creating near-misses), leading to the chaotic feeling you described.\n\nThis is where the difference between a perfectly engineered system and a real-world system becomes stark. A perfectly engineered roundabout is smooth, but if the drivers are operating with high cognitive load—perhaps due to poor sightlines or confusing signage—the system defaults to unpredictable, stop-start behavior. You need",
